 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 52,
   "text": "THE MOURNES. Granite peaks that sweep down to the sea, which is an actual line from a song. Percy French wrote it in 1896. He was an engineer by day and a songwriter by whim."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 37,
   "text": "THE BRANDY PAD. A smugglers' trail across the Mournes, used in the 1700s to carry tea, tobacco and brandy from the coast, past a taxman who never fancied the climb. Today it is mostly walkers carrying sandwiches."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "THE MOURNE WALL. 35 km of drystone wall, built between 1904 and 1922 over 15 summits without a drop of mortar, to keep sheep and cattle out of the reservoir catchment. The sheep have views on this."
  },
  {
   "x": 51,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "SLIEVE DONARD. 850 metres, and the highest peak in Northern Ireland. On a clear day you can see Wales, Scotland and the Isle of Man. On most days you can see your own boots."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 40,
   "text": "SPELGA. A reservoir behind a dam, in the high Mournes. The water is very clear and very cold. Swimming is not recommended, and neither are opinions on whether it is a lake."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "BOG. The brown bit ahead is deep peat. Without proper know-how the ground simply swallows your boot and keeps it. Bog-cross is the old art of walking on it. Come back with the knack."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "THE HOLLOW. Peat takes a thousand years to grow a metre. Nobody here is in a hurry. The bog has all day, and all next year."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 51,
   "plaque": "mournes_narnia",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Narnia's Mountains",
   "text": "C. S. Lewis, who grew up in Belfast, loved the Mournes. He said that the part of Rostrevor overlooking Carlingford Lough was his idea of Narnia. If a lion comes over the ridge, stay calm and be polite."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "mournes_silent_valley",
   "title": "Silent Valley",
   "text": "The Mourne Wall was built to guard the water catchment for Belfast, and in 1933 the Silent Valley reservoir was finished. Water from these hills still runs to taps in Belfast. Have a cup of tea there and you are drinking a little bit of the Mournes."
  },
{
 "x": 45,
 "y": 33,
 "plaque": "arc_ni_wall_builders",
 "title": "The Wall Builders",
 "text": "The Mourne Wall was built between 1904 and 1922 for the Belfast Water Commissioners to keep livestock off the land that drains to Silent Valley. Local men built it by hand from the granite on the hillside, with no mortar. It is about 22 miles long and crosses 15 summits, and the sheep still test it."
},
{
 "x": 38,
 "y": 51,
 "plaque": "arts_percy_french",
 "theme": "arts",
 "title": "The Mountains of Mourne",
 "text": "Percy French, born in Roscommon in 1854, trained as an engineer and worked as an inspector of drains before he found his trade as an entertainer and songwriter. He wrote 'The Mountains of Mourne' in 1896, a letter home from a lad in London: 'where the Mountains of Mourne sweep down to the sea'. He also wrote 'Are Ye Right There, Michael?' about the West Clare Railway, which sued him and lost. He died in 1920. The mountains have never stopped sweeping, and the sea is in no hurry either."
}
],
